2) Not covered by the MIT licence

The following are NOT part of the software and are not licensed with it:

  * The name "Bücherhausen" and its logo, which belong to the blog this was
    written for. An MIT licence covers code and says nothing about names
    either way; this is a clarification rather than a restriction. Do not use
    it as the name of your own shelf and do not suggest that Bücherhausen
    made or endorses your version. Naming it as the origin - which is what
    the credit line in the footer does - is exactly right and always welcome.

    "Mein Regal" is not reserved. It is an ordinary German phrase meaning "my
    shelf", not a name anybody should own. Your shelf is named in config.php
    and the software is named by a constant in the source; change either, or
    both. Call yours whatever you like.

  * Anything in public/assets/brand/ - the operator's own logo and icons.
    That folder is excluded from version control and from the deployment, so
    nothing in it travels with this repository; it belongs to whoever made it.
  * The bundled webfonts, which carry their own licences
    (Inter and IBM Plex Serif are both SIL Open Font License 1.1).
  * public/js/zxing.min.js - @zxing/library, Apache License 2.0.
    See public/js/zxing.README.txt for the version and origin.
  * Book cover photographs and any cover images retrieved from third parties.
    These belong to their respective rights holders. Covers photographed by
    the operator remain the operator's own. Cover images from third-party
    services are linked, not redistributed, and stay subject to the terms of
    the service they come from.
  * Catalogue data. Bibliographic metadata retrieved from the Deutsche
    Nationalbibliothek is published by the DNB under CC0; data from other
    services is subject to those services' terms.

The default logo and icons in public/assets/ are part of the software and are
covered by the MIT licence above. They exist so that a fresh installation has a
mark of its own rather than borrowing somebody else's - which is also why no
other brand ships here. To use your own, put it in public/assets/brand/; see
the README.


3) The credit line, and what it is not

Every installation shows a line in its footer reading "Läuft mit Mein Regal,
von Bücherhausen", linking to the project page and to the blog this was
written for.

Please leave it there. That is a request and not a condition: the MIT licence
above governs copies of the source, and it asks nothing of what a running
installation puts on the screen. Removing the line is permitted, and saying
otherwise here would be a term nobody intends to enforce dressed up as one
somebody might.

The reason to keep it is not legal. This was written for one bookshelf and
given away; the line is how anybody looking at your shelf can find out that
the software exists and where it came from. It is small, it links out rather
than shouting, and it costs nothing that matters.

The one thing that is not a request is the blog's name above: credit
Bücherhausen as where this came from, not as who made your version.
